# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo

# local scratch / screenshots
.tmp-screenshot/
next-env.d.ts

# local render artifact
.blog-render.html

# playwright artifacts
/test-results/
/playwright-report/
/Mascot/
# Build outputs written to an alternate dist dir (.next-verify, .next-e2e, …). Globbed
# rather than listed one-by-one: `.next-verify/` was added alone, then `.next-e2e/` showed
# up untracked at 938 MB — git was offering to commit a build cache, and Tailwind v4 (which
# scans the project directory and skips exactly what .gitignore skips) was free to read it.
# That cache holds turbopack's BINARY .sst files, which do contain byte noise shaped like
# `border-[var(--x)]` — the same shape as a CSS parse failure seen 2026-08-11
# ("Unexpected token Delim('-')" blamed on a globals.css line number that does not exist).
# Causation was never pinned down (the directory was deleted mid-investigation, and a clean
# `next build` passes), so treat that as a strong lead, not a proven root cause. The glob
# stands on its own merit regardless: a dist dir must never be tracked or scanned.
.next-*/
